title: Does a sign become more honest when its maker admits what it doesn't yet do?
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

Even if the label doesn't?

The [[Oka Chair]]'s site says "Designed, Sourced, and Built in the USA." Its maker will tell you the hemp fiber comes from France, and why. See [[Eight Chairs]], [[Hemp]].

A juice carton says "recyclable," and nobody at the company is offering the rest of the story. See [[Tetra Pak]], [[Rethinking the Recycling Symbol]], [[Greenwashing]].

More cases, already in here:

Makers who show the gap:

* [[Solar.LowTechMagazine.com]] shows its battery level and goes dark when it runs out. [[Kris De Decker]] then wrote up how sustainable the solar site really is.
* The [[Eames Molded Plastic Side Chair]] stopped production over its own material, and came back as something re-assessed twice!

Labels that are true and mislead:

* [[Everyone is going carbon neutral these days]]
* "Compostable," on a cup made of [[PLA]]. It is compostable. The label leaves out that it needs an industrial composter to do it, and that it can't go in with ordinary plastic recycling.
* [[Clean Coal]], and [[Are you kidding me?]]
* [[Worrying About Your Carbon Footprint Is Exactly What Big Oil Wants You to Do]]

To find and add: Patagonia's "Don't Buy This Jacket" ad, Interface and Ray Anderson, Fairphone, the numbered triangles on plastics.

Is the sign the object, the label, or the person standing behind it?

See [[Signs Signaling Sustainability]], [[Recyclable, but really?]]
